import{_ as t,o,c as a,a2 as s}from"./chunks/framework.C0pL2NcE.js";const g=JSON.parse('{"title":"Level Editor","description":"","frontmatter":{},"headers":[],"relativePath":"guide/level-editor.md","filePath":"guide/level-editor.md"}'),r={name:"guide/level-editor.md"};function n(l,e,i,d,c,h){return o(),a("div",null,[...e[0]||(e[0]=[s('<h1 id="level-editor" tabindex="-1">Level Editor <a class="header-anchor" href="#level-editor" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Level Editor&quot;">​</a></h1><p>The level editor runs in the browser: <a href="https://tuandds.github.io/colony-flow-sand/demo/editor.html" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">open the Level Editor</a> (or the <strong>Level Editor</strong> button on the landing page). On the dev server: <a href="http://localhost:8080/editor.html" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">http://localhost:8080/editor.html</a>. Its labels are in Vietnamese. It follows the team&#39;s level editor template: an <strong>Editor</strong> mode with four panels and an <strong>Organizer</strong> mode for chapter sets.</p><p>It turns an image into a sand picture, grain by grain, in the game&#39;s own colours, then builds nests that a recorded play wins. It uses the game&#39;s own code (<code>src/game/levels.ts</code>, <code>src/game/sim.ts</code>, <code>src/game/bot.ts</code>), so what it shows is what the game plays.</p><h2 id="toolbar" tabindex="-1">Toolbar <a class="header-anchor" href="#toolbar" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Toolbar&quot;">​</a></h2><table tabindex="0"><thead><tr><th>Button</th><th>What it does</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Editor / Organizer</td><td>Switch mode</td></tr><tr><td>🏠</td><td>Open the game</td></tr><tr><td>▶</td><td>Play the current level in the floating phone panel (<code>index.html?custom=preview</code>)</td></tr><tr><td>🤖</td><td><strong>Auto Play</strong>: replay the recorded winning game in the phone panel (the test bot plays when there is no recording)</td></tr><tr><td>💾</td><td>Save the Level Bank and chapter sets in the browser (<code>Ctrl+S</code>)</td></tr><tr><td>⬆ / ⬇</td><td>Import JSON (one level, a list or an export) / download the current level</td></tr><tr><td>🕘</td><td>Level Bank backups: the last 15 saves, restorable</td></tr></tbody></table><p>The phone panel can be dragged by its title bar, resized from its corner, reloaded or opened in its own window. Each panel can be collapsed (–) and resized by dragging the bar between panels; double-click a bar to reset it.</p><h2 id="_1-colour-bank" tabindex="-1">1. Colour Bank <a class="header-anchor" href="#_1-colour-bank" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;1. Colour Bank&quot;">​</a></h2><ul><li><strong>Active Palette</strong>: the level&#39;s own colours, plus the eraser. Every colour in the picture is always in it. Tap or drag a Bank colour into the box to add one; drag one back onto the Bank to take it off (only if the picture doesn&#39;t use it). Keys <code>1</code>–<code>0</code> pick the first ten. Long-press or right-click a colour to edit it.</li><li><strong>Bank</strong> (&quot;every colour a single-word key&quot;): starts as 24 sand colours (<code>BANK_COLOURS</code> in <code>src/game/levels.ts</code>): colours of the built-in pictures and of the team template&#39;s bank (purple, orchid, lilac, royal, aqua and more), with colours that look alike removed (CIELAB distance under about 20). A Bank saved before gets the new colours added and the removed ones dropped, once; colours you changed or added stay. Pictures that use a removed colour still draw it. Each row has a handle (⠿, drag to reorder), a swatch and its name. Tap a colour to paint with it; long-press or right-click to edit it.</li><li><strong>Edit colour</strong>: change the name (one word, no spaces, unique) or the colour, take it off this level&#39;s Active Palette, or delete it. A colour a picture uses can&#39;t be deleted. A changed colour changes that sand in every level using it, from its next save.</li><li><strong>＋ Add colour</strong> adds a colour to the Bank (up to 52); <strong>↺</strong> resets the Bank to the 24 default colours. The Bank is kept in the browser (<code>cf_editor_bank</code>); each level saves its own colours, so the game shows them as they were when it was saved.</li></ul><h2 id="_2-sand-editor" tabindex="-1">2. Sand Editor <a class="header-anchor" href="#_2-sand-editor" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;2. Sand Editor&quot;">​</a></h2><ul><li>A summary line (<code>colours › blocks › grains</code>) and notes: no sand, more than 12 colours, more than 420 blocks, colours with fewer than 64 grains (they make no block and are cut), and whether the nests are checked.</li><li><strong>Counts</strong>: blocks and grains per colour, after the game&#39;s exact-fit trim.</li><li><strong>🖼️ Create sand picture from an image…</strong> opens the generator (below). You can also drop an image anywhere on the editor or paste one (<code>Ctrl+V</code>).</li><li>The picture is drawn as sand, one pixel per grain, with the in-game grain shading, inside a copy of the game&#39;s frame. The canvas is exactly the frame&#39;s inside: a picture that touches its edge here touches the frame in the game. The frame is the same in every level, 186 × 176 grains (22 cells tall, a little wider than tall), so sand cells are the same size from level to level; a smaller picture sits centred in it. Every 8 × 8 grains is one cell (faint grid); every 64 grains of a colour make one block.</li><li>Tools: ✏️ paint (<code>B</code>), 🧽 erase (<code>E</code>), 🪣 fill a region (<code>G</code>), 🔁 replace a whole colour (<code>R</code>), 💧 pick (<code>I</code>); right-click erases; brush size 2–24 grains (<code>[</code> <code>]</code>); <code>Ctrl+Z</code> / <code>Ctrl+Shift+Z</code> undo and redo.</li><li><strong>Settings</strong>: name, difficulty (nest size, from very easy 6–10 blocks to very hard 12–18), status (WIP / REVIEW / LIVE) and a WIP comment (500 characters), saved with the level.</li></ul><h3 id="generator" tabindex="-1">Generator <a class="header-anchor" href="#generator" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Generator&quot;">​</a></h3><p>Settings: only the Active Palette&#39;s colours (optional), picture size (12–22 cells tall, 96–176 grains, and about 1.11 times as wide; the picture is placed centred in the frame at the game&#39;s fixed scale, so a smaller size makes a smaller picture, not bigger cells), how the image sits in the frame (fit the whole image, or fill the frame and crop), where the picture sits in the frame (the arrow buttons move it a cell, 8 grains, at a time, ⊙ puts it back in the middle, or drag the preview; it stops where its sand would leave the frame, so empty space around the picture doesn&#39;t count), most colours (2–12, the outline not counted), background removal (grains this close in CIELAB to the image&#39;s corner colour become empty; transparent pixels are always empty), speckle smoothing (passes of a 3 × 3 majority filter), and an optional dark outline 4 grains wide (off by default, so the picture keeps the image&#39;s own edge).</p><p>How it works: every grain takes the nearest palette colour in CIELAB, so &quot;near&quot; means &quot;looks alike&quot;. While there are too many colours, it drops the colour whose loss changes the picture least, so a small but distinct detail (a red roof, a sun) is kept. Loose specks smaller than 64 grains are removed, because the game would let them fall at once. The preview shows the result as sand with its block counts; <strong>Apply</strong> replaces the picture.</p><h2 id="_3-nest-editor" tabindex="-1">3. Nest Editor <a class="header-anchor" href="#_3-nest-editor" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;3. Nest Editor&quot;">​</a></h2><ul><li><strong>Nest columns</strong>: 2 to 6 (default 4). The game spreads the columns around the screen centre, closer together past 4. Changing the count drops the current nests, so generate them again.</li><li>A summary line (<code>nests › blocks in nests / blocks in the picture</code>) and a chip per colour showing whether the nests hold exactly the picture&#39;s blocks.</li><li><strong>🤖 Auto-generate nests</strong> finds nests the level can be won with. It runs up to 4 attempts at once in web workers and keeps the first one that wins twice: <ol><li><strong>Recorded play</strong> (8 tries). The bot plays the picture from the bottom up, digging colours an open nest wants first and waiting while the belt is busy. Each column always has a nest. When one is done, the next opens at once for the colour most common on the belt, or about to arrive, or lowest in the picture. A nest that already holds most of its minimum size closes early when the belt gets crowded. The nests and the swipes are saved with the level (<code>nests</code>, <code>moves</code>). The check replays the same swipes against those nests. The game&#39;s rules use a seeded random generator, so the replay plays out exactly like the recording, which proves a winning set of swipes exists.</li><li><strong>Fixed orders</strong>: <code>rows</code> (bottom-up) and <code>onion</code> (outside-in) with seeds 0–5, played by the test bot.</li><li>If nothing wins, the same again with nests one step smaller.</li></ol></li><li>The log shows each attempt; a failed one says whether it failed while recording or while replaying, what the front nests still needed and what was on the belt.</li><li><strong>Edit nests by hand</strong>: click a nest to change its colour or size, move it up/down or to another column, or delete it; ＋ adds a nest to a column. Drag a nest onto another nest to swap the two, or onto a column&#39;s ＋ to move it to the end of that column. Hand edits remove the ✓ and the recorded swipes.</li><li><strong>Advanced</strong>: the fixed order and seed, and a button that drops recorded or hand-made nests.</li></ul><h2 id="_4-level-bank" tabindex="-1">4. Level Bank <a class="header-anchor" href="#_4-level-bank" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;4. Level Bank&quot;">​</a></h2><ul><li>Chips filter the list by chapter set (ALL = the whole bank).</li><li><strong>＋ New Level</strong>, <strong>＋ Chapter Set</strong>, search by name, filters (status, difficulty, check) and undo for bank actions.</li><li>Each row: number, drag handle (drag to reorder), preview, name, status, colours, blocks, check (✓ checked, ! changed since, – not checked), difficulty, version history (the last 10 saved versions of that level), duplicate, delete.</li><li><strong>Save</strong> keeps everything in the browser (<code>localStorage</code>: <code>cf_custom_levels</code>, <code>cf_custom_chapters</code>, plus history and backups); changed game levels also go to the game (see <a href="#game-levels">Game levels</a>). <strong>Play all</strong> opens the game with <code>?custom=1</code>, which plays the bank instead of the built-in levels.</li><li><strong>Export / Import</strong> JSON; <strong>Copy TS</strong> copies the current level as a <code>LevelDef</code> to paste into <code>LEVELS</code> in <code>src/game/levels.ts</code>.</li></ul><h2 id="game-levels" tabindex="-1">Game levels <a class="header-anchor" href="#game-levels" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Game levels&quot;">​</a></h2><p>The game&#39;s 10 built-in levels are in the Level Bank too (ids <code>builtin-0</code> to <code>builtin-9</code>, chapter set <strong>Game</strong>, status LIVE). They are added at the top the first time the editor opens, and saved with the bank on the next <strong>Save</strong>. A level you delete from the bank afterwards is not added again.</p><ul><li>They are converted from the cell pictures in <code>src/game/levels.ts</code> (<code>builtinDefs</code>): the same grains and the same nests (<code>salt</code> is the level&#39;s number, as in the game). Colours match the Colour Bank by hex; the built-in shades the Bank lacks are kept as hidden sand colours (sky2, brown2 and so on), and five that look the same as a Bank colour (CIELAB distance under 6) use that colour.</li><li><strong>Edits reach the game.</strong> The game (<code>index.html</code>, without <code>?custom</code>) plays, for each built-in level, the newest saved copy: this browser&#39;s Level Bank, or <code>src/game/level-edits.json</code>. It reads them each time a level starts, so the game updates on its next level or restart.</li><li><strong>On the dev server</strong>, every Save also writes the built-in levels that play differently from the original (picture, nests, difficulty, order, seed, columns or name) to <code>src/game/level-edits.json</code>, one level per line. Commit that file and publish to put the changes in the online game. A level in the file that is newer than this browser&#39;s copy replaces it when the editor opens (after a <code>git pull</code>, say).</li><li>On the published site the editor cannot write files: changes there reach only the game in the same browser.</li><li>To bring a level back to the original, delete it from the bank and save (or use its version history).</li></ul><h2 id="organizer" tabindex="-1">Organizer <a class="header-anchor" href="#organizer" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Organizer&quot;">​</a></h2><ul><li><strong>Chapter Sets</strong> (left): new, rename (with a description), duplicate, delete, and import a set from JSON (its levels are added to the bank). &quot;All levels&quot; shows the bank itself.</li><li><strong>Chapter Builder</strong> (right): the set&#39;s levels as a table with order, name (click to open in the Editor), preview, blocks, nests, colours, status, difficulty, nest check, Auto Play result and check result. Drag rows to reorder; status and difficulty can be changed in place.</li><li>Buttons: select levels / select all / delete selected (from the set; in &quot;All levels&quot; they are deleted from the bank), add levels, undo, save, <strong>Play chapter</strong> (the set&#39;s levels in order in the phone panel), <strong>Auto Play set</strong> (replays every level&#39;s recorded win in the workers, or lets the bot play), <strong>Check set</strong> (no sand, nests that don&#39;t fit, unchecked nests, too many colours, too large, no name), export CSV and JSON, edit set, delete set.</li></ul><h2 id="level-format" tabindex="-1">Level format <a class="header-anchor" href="#level-format" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Level format&quot;">​</a></h2><p>A custom level is a <code>LevelDef</code> with <code>sand</code> (the whole frame: <code>n</code> = 22 cells, <code>size</code> = 176 grains tall, <code>w</code> = 186 grains wide, see <code>FRAME_N</code> and <code>frameGrains</code> in <code>src/game/layout.ts</code>; a picture of another size is shown centred in the frame at the same scale; <code>data</code> run-length encoded row by row: a colour key followed by its run length, <code>.</code> = empty), <code>colors</code> (key → hex, from <code>SAND_PALETTE</code>), <code>chunk</code>, <code>order</code>, <code>seed</code>, <code>salt</code>, <code>cols</code> (nest columns, 2–6, default 4) and, when a recorded play found them, <code>nests</code> (each column front to back as <code>[colour, blocks]</code>) and <code>moves</code> (the winning swipes as <code>[time, points]</code>). The editor also stores <code>id</code>, <code>status</code>, <code>comment</code>, <code>verified</code> and <code>updated</code>; the game ignores them. The game uses <code>nests</code> only when they hold exactly the picture&#39;s blocks; otherwise it falls back to <code>order</code> and <code>seed</code>. Levels from the first editor version (cell pictures in <code>rows</code>) are converted to grains when opened and need their nests generated again. Levels made at another size before the scale was fixed are placed centred in the 22-cell frame when opened (sand outside it is cut, and the check is marked as changed).</p>',24)])])}const m=t(r,[["render",n]]);export{g as __pageData,m as default};
